// A named byte channel between two workloads inside the project, as the
// vocabulary's `channel` kind.
//
// # It is what v1 called `p2p`, and the rename is the vocabulary's
//
// v2 named this `channel` on purpose: `p2p` names the TRANSPORT rather than the
// thing, the way `http` would be a poor name for a route. What it is, is a
// named, ordered, bidirectional conduit between exactly two workloads inside
// one project, carried by libp2p, with no edge in the path — the way in that is
// not an ingress. So the package is `taubyte:channel` and the kind is
// `channel`, and anyone reading v1's `services/substrate/components/p2p/` is
// reading this.
//
// # The protocol is namespaced by the platform
//
// An author writes a name inside the project — `sync` — and what goes on the
// wire has the project's identity prepended. Two projects that both call a
// channel `sync` never meet, and an author who could write the full wire
// protocol could collide with another tenant on purpose.
//
// # One verb, and it is a write
//
// `dial` is the vocabulary's only op here, and it is classified as a WRITE
// because what travels is opaque: nothing on this side can look at the bytes
// and decide the call only read something.
//
// # Request and response, and the conduit that is not here yet
//
// `dial` sends a body and answers with one. That is the shape almost every use
// of v1's p2p actually had — ask the sidecar, get the answer — and it needs no
// handle that outlives the call.
//
// A GENUINE CONDUIT IS A `resource`, and it is deliberately not here. Streaming
// in both directions means an own<channel> with read and write, which means
// `wasi:io/streams`, which means the thing on the other end has to be able to
// hold a connection open per caller. Declaring that shape before anything
// serves it would publish a promise no backing keeps — and the request/response
// half is not a stepping stone to it that has to be thrown away: `dial` stays
// exactly as it reads when a `connect` returning a resource lands beside it.
package taubyte:channel@0.1.0;

interface dialer {
    variant error {
        // The label is not in this resource's `channel:` block.
        no-such-channel(string),
        // Declared, but not with `dial`.
        denied(string),
        // Nothing is serving the channel: no node in this deployment could be
        // found to run the workload that answers it, or the channel names a
        // workload that is not in the project. Its own arm because a caller
        // RETRIES this — the name is right and the far end is not there yet,
        // which on a cold deploy is a matter of seconds.
        no-answer(string),
        // The far end answered and what it said was a failure.
        failed(string),
        unavailable(string),
    }

    // dial sends one message to whatever serves the channel and returns its
    // answer. It is bounded by the CALLER's own timeout: a channel declares no
    // deadline of its own, and the workload waiting is the one whose budget is
    // being spent.
    dial: func(name: string, body: list<u8>) -> result<list<u8>, error>;
}

world imports {
    import dialer;
}
